<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      trade_order: the two time-based exits, snapshotted at entry.

      WHY SNAPSHOT AND NOT READ THE CONFIG
      ====================================
      Exactly the reasoning behind 011 (target_at_entry / stop_loss_at_entry) and
      036 (trail_ladder_at_entry), and it applies with more force here:

        1. PositionService must not depend on SharedData still being populated.
           It walks trade_order rows, and in live mode a restart mid-session
           leaves the config caches empty while positions are still open. A
           time stop that silently stops existing because a cache was cold is
           worse than no time stop.

        2. Editing a config mid-session must not re-time trades already open.
           Shortening max_hold_minutes from 90 to 60 at 13:00 would otherwise
           instantly breach - and close - every position opened before 12:00,
           which is a mass liquidation triggered by a form submit.

      BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL FOR EVERY EXISTING TRADE
      ==========================================
      Both columns are nullable and PositionService reads null as "this exit does
      not apply". Rows written before this changeset have null in both, so no
      historical trade acquires an exit it did not have, and no config that omits
      the 042 columns produces one. Strategies 1-4 are unaffected.

      WHY flatten_at IS A TIME AND NOT A TIMESTAMP
      ===========================================
      It is a time-of-day rule ("close everything at 15:15"), and the trade
      already carries its own entry_time, so the date is never ambiguous: these
      are intraday strategies and forceCloseOpenPositions squares off the same
      session. Storing a full timestamp would invite a row whose flatten moment
      is on a different day from its entry, which is not a thing this system can
      mean.

      TWO NEW exit_reason VALUES
      ==========================
      TIME_STOP  - max_hold_minutes elapsed
      FLATTEN    - flatten_at reached

      Both are distinct from the existing FORCE_CLOSE, which is the replay's
      end-of-day sweep (15:20) and means "the run ended with this still open".
      Collapsing them would make it impossible to tell a strategy that exits on
      its own clock from one that had to be cleaned up after, which is exactly
      the distinction a 90-minute-max-hold engine is measured on. exit_reason is
      a free-text column, so no schema change is needed for the values
      themselves - they are listed here because this is the changeset that
      introduces them and docs/ORDERS_AND_POSITIONS.md points back at it.
    -->
    <changeSet id="043_add_time_exits_to_trade_order" author="moneymaker">
        <!-- Same ddl-auto=update guard as 035 / 041 / 042. -->
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="trade_order" columnName="max_hold_minutes_at_entry"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <addColumn tableName="trade_order">
            <!--
              Minutes from entry_time after which PositionService closes the
              trade with exit_reason = TIME_STOP. NULL = no time stop.
            -->
            <column name="max_hold_minutes_at_entry" type="INT"/>
            <!--
              Time-of-day at which PositionService closes the trade with
              exit_reason = FLATTEN regardless of P&L. NULL = no flatten.
            -->
            <column name="flatten_at_entry" type="TIME"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
